      "heading": "Three multi-agent patterns worth knowing",
      "narration": "Multi-agent means several model instances with different prompts and different tools, cooperating on one task. Three patterns cover almost everything people actually build, and it is worth being precise about why they help, because the usual explanation is wrong. The supervisor pattern is the most useful one. A router agent receives the task, works out which specialist should handle it, and delegates — with each specialist having its own focused prompt and its own small set of tools. A support system might route to a billing agent, a technical agent and an account agent. This is the pattern that most often earns its complexity, and the reason is not that the agents collaborate cleverly. It is scoping. Each specialist has a short prompt about one domain and four tools instead of a long prompt about six domains and twenty tools, and a shorter prompt with fewer available wrong choices is measurably more reliable. Sequential pipelines are the second pattern: agent A researches, agent B drafts from the research, agent C edits the draft. Each step is specialised and the handoffs are explicit. This is predictable, testable, and easy to debug because you can inspect the artifact between any two stages. It is also, honestly, a workflow with model calls in it — and I mean that as praise rather than criticism, because that is exactly what you should prefer when the sequence is knowable. Debate and critique is the third. One agent produces something, a second attacks it, and either the first revises or a third adjudicates. This genuinely improves quality on open-ended work — arguments get stronger when something looks for holes in them, and the effect is real and measurable on writing and analysis. It also costs three or more times as much and takes three times as long, so it belongs on work where quality matters more than latency and where a human would otherwise be doing the reviewing. Now the sentence to take away, because it corrects the most common misconception in this area. The benefit of multi-agent systems is scoping, not emergent intelligence. Five agents do not think better together than one agent thinks alone — they are the same model, and nothing about running it five times creates a capability that was not there. What multi-agent buys you is that each instance has a narrower job, a shorter prompt, a smaller tool set, and therefore fewer ways to go wrong. Every real benefit you will measure traces back to that. Which is a useful thing to know, because it tells you when the pattern will not help: if your task cannot be cleanly divided into narrower jobs, adding agents adds coordination cost and buys you nothing.",
