      "heading": "The costs the demo leaves out",
      "narration": "Multi-agent systems demo extremely well, and the demo omits four costs that decide whether one is a good idea. Cost, first, and it multiplies faster than people expect. Each agent has its own system prompt, its own tool schemas, and its own conversation history, and every one of those is tokens on every call that agent makes. A five-agent system working on one task frequently uses five to ten times the tokens of a single well-scoped agent doing the same job, and the supervisor pattern adds another layer because the router is itself a model call. If your justification for the architecture is quality, measure whether you got quality proportional to a tenfold bill. Reliability, second, and it is the same arithmetic that limited long agent loops, now applied sideways. If each agent in a chain is ninety percent reliable, five of them in sequence give you fifty-nine percent. Adding agents adds steps, and steps multiply. So a multi-agent system is not more robust than a single agent by virtue of having more parts — it is usually less robust, and each additional specialist has to earn its place by removing more error than it introduces. Debugging, third, and this is the one that hurts in practice. With a single agent, something goes wrong and you read the trace: here is what it thought, here is what it called, here is what came back. With five agents you first have to work out which agent produced the bad output, then what it was actually handed by the agent before it, then whether the fault was in that agent's reasoning or in the handoff. The trace is now several interleaved traces, and reproducing the failure requires reproducing the whole choreography. This is why good tracing is not optional for multi-agent work — it is the only thing standing between you and guessing. Fourth, and it is the subtlest: information loss at the handoffs. Agent A finished its research and wrote a summary for agent B. Somewhere in the material A read was a caveat it did not think worth including. B never sees it, produces a confident answer that the caveat would have changed, and nothing in the system reports that anything was lost — because from B's perspective nothing was. Every handoff is a lossy compression step performed by a model that does not know what the next agent will need. Adding agents adds handoffs, and each one is a place where something quietly falls out of the system. So the discipline is: start with one agent, and add a second only when you can name the specific failure it fixes and measure that the fix worked. Growing a multi-agent system by intuition produces something expensive, fragile and unexplainable, and it usually performs worse than the single agent it replaced.",
