      "heading": "Enies Lobby: nine people, one objective",
      "narration": "Enies Lobby is a multi-agent system, and it is a useful one to think with because it shows both halves — why the pattern works and how it nearly comes apart. Watch the structure. The crew arrives at the gate and immediately splits, and they do not regroup until the objective is achieved. Zoro takes the swordsman. Sanji takes the one he can reach fastest. Chopper handles what needs a doctor's knowledge, Nami takes the one who fights with weather, Usopp takes the one nobody expected him to take. Nine people, one objective, and after the first minute almost no communication between them. That is exactly what a multi-agent system is, and stating it that plainly already tells you where the risks are. Now, why does it work? Not because they coordinate brilliantly, because they do not coordinate at all. It works because the division of labour is clean. Nobody has to hold a meeting about who takes the swordsman — the assignment falls out of who each of them is. Each crew member has a narrow, well-understood capability and takes the sub-problem that matches it, and there is no ambiguity to resolve. That is the supervisor pattern with a perfect router: a specialist per task, scoped tightly, chosen without deliberation. When your problem decomposes that cleanly, multi-agent is genuinely the right architecture, and the reason it helps is the reason from the last slide — each of them is doing one narrow thing they are suited for, not because the group is smarter than its members. Now watch where it nearly falls apart, because that is the more useful half. Almost nobody knows what anyone else has found. Robin learns something crucial and there is no way to tell the others. Decisions get made on information that was true twenty minutes ago. People fight battles that are already unnecessary because the situation changed elsewhere. Every one of those is a handoff failure, and they are not caused by anyone being wrong — they are caused by there being no channel. And the thing that actually holds the operation together is one piece of shared state that everybody can see from wherever they are: the flag burning above the building. It is a single signal, globally visible, that tells every one of them the objective is still live and everyone else is still committed. It is not communication. It is shared state. So the lesson to take is not that splitting up is powerful, which is the lesson the demo version of this teaches. It is narrower and more useful: splitting up works when the split is obvious enough that nobody has to negotiate it, and when there is one piece of shared state that every participant can read. Take away the clean division and you get people duplicating each other's work. Take away the shared signal and you get nine agents improvising against a picture of the world that stopped being true.",
